   ("stephenson-nonfiction", "NEAL STEPHENSON · NONFICTION & SHORTER WORKS · NSF", "#6a8a5a", "Neal Stephenson · 1999–2012 · 4 emergents", "Neal Stephenson · Nonfiction & Shorter Works (NSF) — Neal Stephenson, 1999–2012. Stephenson's nonfiction and short fiction: computing as culture, undersea cables as civilization's nervous system, and an engineer's parable about building a 20-kilometer tower to the edge of space. THE BIG IDEA: The command line, the undersea cable, the tower to the edge of space: three arguments that the future is built, not predicted. 4 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("player-piano", "PLAYER PIANO · PNP", "#b6af9e", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1952 · 8 emergents", "Player Piano (PNP) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1952. An automated society has made human labor obsolete — the machines play the music now, and the men who used to play it have nothing left to do. THE THEME: The machines play the music now. The first of his closed worlds — and the only one he'd spend a career prying open. THE PURPLE THREAD: Introduces ILIUM, NY — the recurring Vonnegut city (it returns in Cat's Cradle and Slaughterhouse-Five). EPICAC itself is a callback to his 1950 short story 'EPICAC.' No human recurring cast originates here yet. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("the-sirens-of-titan", "THE SIRENS OF TITAN · SOT", "#4aa0c8", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1959 · 8 emergents", "The Sirens of Titan (SOT) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1959. All of human history turns out to have been manipulated to deliver a trivial spare part to a stranded alien — and meaning collapses into 'to love whoever is around to be loved.' THE THEME: History as someone else's errand. The funniest book ever written about the heat-death of meaning — and the smallest, kindest answer to it. THE PURPLE THREAD: TRALFAMADORE and the Tralfamadorians DEBUT here (as robots — re-imagined as 4-D beings in Slaughterhouse-Five). The RUMFOORD surname begins here (Winston Niles); Bertram Copeland Rumfoord in Slaughterhouse-Five is the same family. Kazak the hound reappears in Breakfast of Champions. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("mother-night", "MOTHER NIGHT · MN1", "#6a6a82", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1961 · 7 emergents", "Mother Night (MN1) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1961. A spy who broadcast Nazi propaganda as cover finds the mask and the man have become indistinguishable — 'we are what we pretend to be, so we must be careful about what we pretend to be.' THE THEME: The one with a stated moral, because he was afraid we'd miss it: be careful what you pretend to be, because that is what you are. THE PURPLE THREAD: HOWARD W. CAMPBELL JR. DEBUTS here — and cameos in Slaughterhouse-Five, where (as the same American Nazi propagandist) he tries to recruit US POWs into a 'Free American Corps.' The principal recurring link. 7 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("cats-cradle", "CAT'S CRADLE · CC1", "#6fd0e0", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1963 · 8 emergents", "Cat's Cradle (CC1) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1963. Science without conscience meets a religion built on cheerful lies — humanity ends not with a bang but with a frozen crystal, and the only solace is foma, comforting untruths. THE THEME: A religion that admits it is lies, and a science that ends the world by accident. Busy, busy, busy. THE PURPLE THREAD: Reuses the recurring setting ILIUM, NY (the Hoenikkers' home). Bokonon, ice-nine, the Hoenikkers & San Lorenzo are unique to this book — but its coinages (foma, karass, granfalloon, wampeter) leak into Vonnegut's NONFICTION, naming the 1974 collection Wampeters, Foma & Granfalloons. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("god-bless-you-mr-rosewater", "GOD BLESS YOU, MR. ROSEWATER · GBR", "#d4af37", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1965 · 8 emergents", "God Bless You, Mr. Rosewater (GBR) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1965. A fortune-heir tries to give away love and money to the worthless and is declared insane for it — asking whether unconditional charity is sanity or madness. THE THEME: “There's only one rule that I know of, babies — God damn it, you've got to be kind.” THE PURPLE THREAD: TWO debuts on the Purple Thread: ELIOT ROSEWATER and KILGORE TROUT both first appear here. Trout (the obscure SF author Eliot reveres) becomes Vonnegut's most pervasive recurring figure; Eliot returns in Slaughterhouse-Five and Breakfast of Champions. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("slaughterhouse-five", "SLAUGHTERHOUSE-FIVE · SH5", "#b0392f", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1969 · 8 emergents", "Slaughterhouse-Five (SH5) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1969. The firebombing of Dresden and the impossibility of narrating atrocity — a traumatized man comes unstuck in time and learns to accept death as just another moment. So it goes. THE THEME: All moments, past, present, and future, always have existed, always will exist. So it goes. THE PURPLE THREAD: The richest node on the Purple Thread. KILGORE TROUT appears (Billy meets him in Ilium); ELIOT ROSEWATER appears (shares Billy's ward, hands him Trout's books); HOWARD W. CAMPBELL JR. appears (recruiting POWs); BERTRAM COPELAND RUMFOORD appears (the recurring family); the TRALFAMADORIANS return from Sirens of Titan; ILIUM, NY recurs. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("breakfast-of-champions", "BREAKFAST OF CHAMPIONS · BOC", "#e0552e", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1973 · 7 emergents", "Breakfast of Champions (BOC) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1973. A man reads a Kilgore Trout novel literally — believing he alone has free will and everyone else is a robot — and the author steps into his own book to set his characters free. THE THEME: The book where the author walks on stage, meets his oldest creation, and lets him go. The asterisk is the whole human animal, reduced to one mark. THE PURPLE THREAD: KILGORE TROUT is the co-lead — his most prominent appearance. RABO KARABEKIAN appears (he later headlines Bluebeard). ELIOT ROSEWATER is referenced (a Trout patron). KAZAK the hound returns from Sirens of Titan. At the end, Vonnegut frees Trout — releasing his own recurring cast. 7 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("slapstick", "SLAPSTICK · SLP", "#5a9a52", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1976 · 6 emergents", "Slapstick (SLP) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1976. Loneliness is America's true disease, curable only by artificial extended families — give everyone a new middle name, and strangers become instant kin. THE THEME: A slogan for a cure: Lonesome No More! Love is too strong a word — but common decency, distributed by lottery, might just do. THE PURPLE THREAD: Largely self-contained — no major recurring cast in central roles. It belongs to the Purple Thread by family resemblance: the same future-decay America, the same humanist ache, that runs through the whole Vonnegutverse. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("jailbird", "JAILBIRD · JBD", "#7a8a9a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1979 · 6 emergents", "Jailbird (JBD) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1979. American guilt and the long betrayal of the left — from a labor massacre to Sacco & Vanzetti to Watergate — refracted through one small, decent bungler just out of prison. THE THEME: The history of American shame, carried by a man too gentle to have earned any of it. Sacco and Vanzetti as the country's recurring sin. THE PURPLE THREAD: A sly Purple-Thread twist: KILGORE TROUT appears here only as a PEN NAME — cellmate Dr. Robert Fender writes SF as 'Kilgore Trout.' ELIOT ROSEWATER / the Rosewater Foundation are folded into the RAMJAC orbit, tying back to God Bless You, Mr. Rosewater. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("deadeye-dick", "DEADEYE DICK · DED", "#a05a4a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1982 · 6 emergents", "Deadeye Dick (DED) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1982. Lifelong guilt for one accidental act, and the neutering of a life — mirrored by the neutron bomb that empties a city while leaving the buildings standing. THE THEME: A life is a peephole the universe briefly opens and then closes. Rudy fires one shot at twelve and spends the rest of his looking through it. THE PURPLE THREAD: Set in MIDLAND CITY, OHIO — the same town as Breakfast of Champions; the HOOVER surname (cf. Dwayne Hoover) recurs. The Grand Hotel Oloffson in Haiti recurs across his late work. A geography-thread, not a character one. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("galapagos", "GALÁPAGOS · GAL", "#3aa088", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1985 · 7 emergents", "Galápagos (GAL) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1985. The oversized human brain is the root of all our trouble — so evolution's fix, over a million years, is simply to shrink it. THE THEME: One million years on, the verdict is in: the trouble was never the heart. It was the big, big brain. THE PURPLE THREAD: The most important late Purple-Thread link: the ghost-narrator LEON TROTSKY TROUT is KILGORE TROUT'S SON. Kilgore appears as the voice in the 'blue tunnel' to the afterlife, repeatedly tempting Leon to cross over. 7 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("bluebeard", "BLUEBEARD · BLB", "#4a6ec0", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1987 · 6 emergents", "Bluebeard (BLB) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1987. Art, authenticity, and the one locked door — what a life's real masterpiece turns out to be, hidden where no one was allowed to look. THE THEME: Behind the one locked door of an abstract-expressionist's life: not abstraction at all, but every soul of a war, painted true. THE PURPLE THREAD: RABO KARABEKIAN recurs from Breakfast of Champions (1973) — there a minor painter whose work delivered the 'band of light' epiphany; here his full life. The clearest single-character bridge on the Purple Thread. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("hocus-pocus", "HOCUS POCUS · HPC", "#8a6a4a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1990 · 6 emergents", "Hocus Pocus (HPC) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1990. America's betrayal of its own ideals — education, class, Vietnam, and prison — in a privatized, segregated near-future, told on scraps of paper. THE THEME: Hocus pocus: the magic words a country says over its own ideals to make them disappear. THE PURPLE THREAD: Largely self-contained on the fictional thread — but it carries Vonnegut's REAL-WORLD recurring heroes: its narrator is named EUGENE DEBS HARTKE, after the socialist Eugene V. Debs, who also turns up in God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("timequake", "TIMEQUAKE · TQK", "#5a7cd0", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1997 · 6 emergents", "Timequake (TQK) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1997. When a cosmic glitch forces everyone to re-live the 1990s on autopilot, only conscious effort — 'ting-a-ling!' — makes us human again. His last novel, half memoir. THE THEME: “You were sick, but now you're well again, and there's work to do.” The mantra he left us with, dressed as a timequake. THE PURPLE THREAD: The capstone of the Purple Thread. KILGORE TROUT gets his largest, most heroic role — an 'absurd messiah' — and Vonnegut stages his death (2001, at the Xanadu clambake). The book is a farewell to his whole recurring universe. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("welcome-to-the-monkey-house", "WELCOME TO THE MONKEY HOUSE · MNK", "#c85a7a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1968 · 6 emergents", "Welcome to the Monkey House (MNK) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1968. Vonnegut's canonical short-fiction sampler — twenty-five stories of technology, conformity, and stubborn decency, 1950–1968. THE THEME: Twenty-five proofs that he could do in ten pages what most can't in three hundred: make you laugh, then break your heart, then make you behave. THE PURPLE THREAD: An anthology, mostly standalone — and a Purple-Thread caution: HARRISON BERGERON, Vonnegut's most-anthologized creation, is a ONE-OFF, not part of the recurring web. The collection predates the Trout-heavy continuity. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("wampeters-foma-and-granfalloons", "WAMPETERS, FOMA & GRANFALLOONS · WFG", "#c8a24a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1974 · 6 emergents", "Wampeters, Foma & Granfalloons (WFG) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1974. His first nonfiction gathering — essays, speeches, and reviews filtered through the Bokononist vocabulary he invented for Cat's Cradle. THE THEME: He titled his opinions in the language of a religion he made up — admitting, gently, that all opinions are a little bit foma. THE PURPLE THREAD: The Purple Thread crossing into nonfiction: the title itself is BOKONONIST vocabulary from Cat's Cradle (wampeter / foma / granfalloon), importing his invented religion into his own essayist's voice. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("palm-sunday", "PALM SUNDAY · PSY", "#8a9a5a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1981 · 5 emergents", "Palm Sunday (PSY) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1981. A self-portrait in collage — essays, letters, a sermon, family history, and the famous report card where he grades his own books. THE THEME: The man graded his own life's work on a report card — and gave himself a D where he'd earned it. Honesty as a literary form. THE PURPLE THREAD: Nonfiction; the recurring subject is VONNEGUT'S OWN BIBLIOGRAPHY — the report card grades every prior novel (Cat's Cradle A+, Slaughterhouse-Five A+, Slapstick D), a meta-commentary running across the whole Vonnegutverse. 5 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("a-man-without-a-country", "A MAN WITHOUT A COUNTRY · MWC", "#b04438", "Kurt Vonnegut · 2005 · 6 emergents", "A Man Without a Country (MWC) — Kurt Vonnegut, 2005. A bitter, funny late-life farewell to America — humor as the last defense against political despair, from a man watching his country lose its mind. THE THEME: “We are here on Earth to fart around, and don't let anybody tell you any different.” The last laugh of a heartbroken patriot. THE PURPLE THREAD: Nonfiction, his final book — carrying the REAL-WORLD recurring heroes of the Purple Thread: EUGENE V. DEBS and MARK TWAIN, the lifelong models who also surface in Hocus Pocus and God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("happy-birthday-wanda-june", "HAPPY BIRTHDAY, WANDA JUNE · WJN", "#d05a8a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1970 · 6 emergents", "Happy Birthday, Wanda June (WJN) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1970. A satire of toxic heroic masculinity — Odysseus's homecoming retold as a violence-loving hunter returning to a world that has moved on without him. THE THEME: The warrior comes home a hero and finds the house has changed the locks. Heaven, meanwhile, is a shuffleboard court. THE PURPLE THREAD: Self-contained — but on the Purple Thread by device: its HEAVEN scenes (the cheerful dead on a shuffleboard court) prefigure the afterlife Vonnegut would reuse in Galápagos's blue tunnel and God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian. He graded the play a D. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
   ("god-bless-you-dr-kevorkian", "GOD BLESS YOU, DR. KEVORKIAN · KEV", "#5a8a9a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1999 · 6 emergents", "God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian (KEV) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1999. Interviews with the dead — Vonnegut takes controlled near-death trips down the blue tunnel to interview the famous departed in a Heaven that has no Hell. THE THEME: He had himself nearly killed, again and again, just to interview the dead — and came back each time with a joke and a kindness. THE PURPLE THREAD: On the Purple Thread to the end: KILGORE TROUT is interviewed in the afterlife ('the ever-present Kilgore Trout'), and the BLUE TUNNEL is the same conduit as Galápagos. EUGENE V. DEBS and ISAAC ASIMOV (his real predecessor as honorary head of the Humanists) appear among the dead. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked."),
